 130	  schedulers — the server's `ConsumerConfig.BackOff` and the handler's own
 131	  `NakWithDelay` — and setting both does not pick one: the server stretches
 132	  each NAK delay by the BackOff increments, so the configured ladder becomes
 133	  dead config that still reads as authoritative (ENT-1535 measured a real
 134	  34h22m envelope against a documented 17h45m). `Config.BackOff` and
 135	  `Config.Retry` are therefore mutually exclusive — a `Start` error — and
 136	  adopting a `Retry` clears any ladder already on the durable.
 137	
 138	  Never a drop, and never a bare `Term`. A failed capture Naks instead, so the
 139	  message survives and the stall stays visible — and `CaptureReserve` holds
 140	  back deliveries specifically to retry it. When even those are spent the
 141	  message is reported as `OutcomeStranded`, not dressed up as a retry: a Nak
 142	  at the broker's cap is dropped, so nothing will touch it again and it needs
 143	  the break-glass runbook. Alert on that outcome.
 144	- **`jsconsumer.FloorMonitor`** — the durable's ack-floor health signal,
 145	  separate from disposition on purpose: ack-floor state is consumer-global
 146	  while giving up on a message is message-local, so this type observes and
 147	  reports and something else decides what that is worth. `Start` polls it and
 148	  `Stop` joins the poll. Publish `FloorStall` as a gauge, drive your own
 149	  quarantine rule off `Stalled()` + `Retry.DeadLetter`, or hand it to
 150	  `RetryConfig.Monitor` and let the breaker act on it.
 151	
 152	  It triggers on **floor-stall age**, not message stream-age: the two diverge
 153	  badly under backlog, where 35 minutes of receipt→first-delivery lag on a
 154	  perfectly healthy message would read as a stall. Stalled is also not the
 155	  same as *stationary* — the clock runs only while the consumer has delivered
 156	  past its own floor, so an idle consumer's motionless floor never accrues
 157	  time to charge the next arriving message with. Once the floor has been
 158	  stalled past `FloorAge` (default 15m), `Settle` captures the raw message and
 159	  headers to the DLQ and Acks the original, well before the ladder would.
 160	
 161	  It does **not** try to name the blocker. JetStream's consumer info cannot
 162	  identify it: `AckFloor.Stream+1` is the blocker only while the server last
 163	  computed the floor from a pending scan — after a drain the floor jumps to
 164	  the delivered high-water mark, so a poison message arriving behind
 165	  non-matching subjects sits well above it (measured on a live server: floor
 166	  2, blocker at stream 6), which any filtered consumer hits routinely. And
 167	  `AckFloor.Consumer+1` is worse: the server only recomputes that floor when
 168	  the message *at* the floor is acked, so it pins to the blocker's first
 169	  delivery and goes stale the moment it is redelivered. Instead the breaker
 170	  requires three independently-true facts — the consumer is stalled, this
 171	  message is above the ack floor, and this message has itself been failing at
 172	  least `FloorAge` (from its own delivery count against the ladder). Gap-proof,
 173	  redelivery-proof, and no inference about which message is responsible.
 174	
 175	  Elapsed failure time is **measured, not inferred**. Summing the ladder rungs
 176	  a message "must have" served assumes every redelivery came from a
 177	  `NakWithDelay`, and JetStream also increments `NumDelivered` on AckWait
 178	  expiry — a panicking handler can burn four deliveries in milliseconds, which
 179	  the arithmetic would score as three rungs. The clock starts at the first
 180	  failure this process observes; it resets on restart, which makes the breaker
 181	  late rather than early.
 182	
 183	  It claims **one message per stall window**, and the window resets when the
 184	  ack floor moves. That is what keeps it a poison-message remedy: under a mass
 185	  transient (entiredb down for an hour) every failing message satisfies every
 186	  condition, and an unrationed breaker would empty the consumer into the DLQ —
 187	  exactly the outcome a short ladder gives, and exactly what this exists to
 188	  avoid. Successive floor-holders still drain immediately, because clearing
 189	  the real blocker advances the floor and re-arms the breaker at once.
 190	
 191	  Ladder and threshold are validated as **one schedule**: `RecoverBy` states
 192	  the delivery by which a transient is expected to clear, and construction
 193	  rejects a ladder whose cumulative wait to it reaches the threshold — the
 194	  breaker must never be able to preempt a message's own recovery window.
 195	
 196	  Acting on it is still a judgement over consumer-global state, so the
 197	  `Breaker` mode's zero value is `BreakerObserve`: evaluate and report the
 198	  trip, act on nothing. Handing a `Retry` a monitor buys the measurement,
 199	  never an automatic disposition; `BreakerEnforce` is an explicit opt-in, and
 200	  enforcing without a monitor is rejected at construction.
 201	
 202	  **Explicit non-goal:** a handler that *dies* on the poison message — panics,
 203	  OOMs — rather than returning an error. `Settle` is the only entry point to
 204	  every disposition the package owns, so such a message is not settled by any
 205	  of them; that follows from the callback contract, not from the breaker.
 206	  Recovering the panic to Ack the message would hide the bug and leave the
 207	  handler's state unreconciled, against the module's existing posture that a
 208	  panicking loop is fatal (`ShutdownGroup`). The stall stays loud — the
 209	  monitor polls independently of message flow and the ack-floor monitor still
 210	  pages — it just isn't auto-remediated.
 211	- **`backoff`** — the redelivery policy for transiently-failed deliveries: a
 212	  `NakWithDelay` envelope — flat by default, optionally growing per delivery
 213	  (`Factor`/`MaxDelay`) — bounded by MaxDeliver, with opt-in
 214	  Term-on-final-delivery so a work-queue message is removed cleanly instead of
 215	  orphaning un-acked. It owns disposition and delay calculation only
 216	  (`NakOrTerm` over `jetstream.Msg`). MaxDeliver mirrors `jetstream.ConsumerConfig`: a
 217	  non-positive value (`0` or `UnlimitedMaxDeliver`) means unlimited redeliveries,
 218	  so bridge an unset `jsconsumer` consumer with its `EffectiveMaxDeliver()`
 219	  rather than the raw field.
