   144	- **`jsconsumer.FloorMonitor`** — the durable's ack-floor health signal,
   145	  separate from disposition on purpose: ack-floor state is consumer-global
   146	  while giving up on a message is message-local, so this type observes and
   147	  reports and something else decides what that is worth. `Start` polls it and
   148	  `Stop` joins the poll. Publish `FloorStall` as a gauge, drive your own
   149	  quarantine rule off `Stalled()` + `Retry.DeadLetter`, or hand it to
   150	  `RetryConfig.Monitor` and let the breaker act on it.
   151	
   152	  It triggers on **floor-stall age**, not message stream-age: the two diverge
   153	  badly under backlog, where 35 minutes of receipt→first-delivery lag on a
   154	  perfectly healthy message would read as a stall. Stalled is also not the
   155	  same as *stationary* — the clock runs only while the consumer has delivered
   156	  past its own floor, so an idle consumer's motionless floor never accrues
   157	  time to charge the next arriving message with. Once the floor has been
   158	  stalled past `FloorAge` (default 15m), `Settle` captures the raw message and
   159	  headers to the DLQ and Acks the original, well before the ladder would.
   160	
   161	  It does **not** try to name the blocker. JetStream's consumer info cannot
   162	  identify it: `AckFloor.Stream+1` is the blocker only while the server last
   163	  computed the floor from a pending scan — after a drain the floor jumps to
   164	  the delivered high-water mark, so a poison message arriving behind
   165	  non-matching subjects sits well above it (measured on a live server: floor
   166	  2, blocker at stream 6), which any filtered consumer hits routinely. And
